# START — Your first program

File: guide/Use.oot
User wiki. Canonical URL: https://openooda.org/guide/#Use.oot

You run your first `.oo` program on this page.
[Home](Home.oot) is the one-minute picture.
[Language](Language.oot) is the syntax.
[Commands](Options.oot) is the full `ooda` list.
[Packages](Packages.oot) is how you add a library.
[Capabilities](Safety.oot) is how an effect gets a token.
[How it works](Limits.oot) is the product path.
[Research](Research.oot) is the design library.

## 1. What you need

You need a checkout of `ooda`.
You need host `gcc`.
That is enough to rebuild the compiler and run a file.

The rebuild is a [self-hosted ecosystem](/research/#RP-5-1-self-hosted-ecosystem.oot).
The tools you type after that are the [pure product CLI](/research/#RP-5-1a-pure-product-compiler-cli.oot).

## 2. Install

From a checkout you already have, rebuild, then install onto XDG:

```
cd ooda
OODAC_BIN=./bootstrap/seed/oodac bash bootstrap/oodac_pure_build oodac/main.oo oodac/oodac
OODA_FS_WRITEDIR="$HOME" OODA_CHECKOUT="$PWD" ooda install
. ~/.config/ooda/env
```

`ooda install` copies `ooda`, `oodac`, `std`, and `runtime` under `~/.local` and writes `~/.config/ooda/env`.
The first machine still needs this tree, host `gcc`, and the seed rebuild.
The payload at https://openooda.org/install is `.oo`. It is not a shell script.

## 3. Official rebuild

`bootstrap/oodac_pure_build` is the official rebuild.
It starts `gcc` and the seed compiler.
After the rebuild in section 2:

```
./oodac/oodac check oodac/main.oo
```

The emit path is the [C backend](/research/#RP-4-x-backend-c-product-floor.oot).
You typecheck, emit C, compile that C with host `gcc`, and run the binary.
That is also what `ooda run` does. See [How it works](Limits.oot).

## 4. PATH

Put `./bin` on your `PATH` for the short `ooda` command:

```
export PATH="$PWD/bin:$PATH"
ooda version
```

Stay in the `ooda` tree when you export this, so `$PWD/bin` is `ooda/bin`.

## 5. Write hello.oo

Save this as `hello.oo`:

```
pub fn greet(name: String) -> String {
    return "Hello, " + name + "!";
}

verify greet {
    assert_eq!(greet("World"), "Hello, World!");
}

pub fn main() {
    println(greet("openOODA"));
}
```

`pub fn` is a public function.
`verify` is a test block next to the function.
`main` is the entry you run.

The checkout also has `fixtures/hello.oo` if you want a file that already exists.

Read [Language](Language.oot) for more of the grammar.
An effect needs a capability token. That story is on [Capabilities](Safety.oot).

## 6. Check, run, test

Typecheck only:

```
ooda check hello.oo
```

Typecheck, emit C, compile with `gcc`, run:

```
ooda run hello.oo
```

`ooda run` prints the program and skips `verify`.

Run `verify` and asserts:

```
ooda test hello.oo
```

`ooda test` runs `verify` and sets `OODA_TEST=1`.
A failing assert fails the command.

JSON errors for an editor or a script:

```
ooda check hello.oo --json-errors
```

`check` is the fast loop. That is [sub-second feedback](/research/#RP-ES-4-es-subsecond-feedback.oot).
The full command list is on [Commands](Options.oot).

## 7. Fix a file

`ooda fix` patches these error classes:

- missing import
- missing `}`
- type errors
- unused `Result`
- discarded `Result` wrap

It asks unless you pass `--yes`.
Writes go through `write_file`.
You need `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`.
Absolute paths and symlinks are refused.

```
export OODA_FS_WRITEDIR="$PWD"
ooda fix hello.oo --yes
```

This is the human [HITL / fix loop](/research/#RP-5-6-hitl-testing.oot).
The edits are [surgical AST patching](/research/#RP-2-1-surgical-ast-patching.oot).
JSON errors plus `ooda fix` is the Observe / Act pair.

See [Capabilities](Safety.oot) for why writes stay inside `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`.

## 8. After the first run

You now have a compiler, a file, a check, a run, and a test.
Add a library from [Packages](Packages.oot).
Pick a backend or a flag from [Commands](Options.oot).
Read [How it works](Limits.oot) when you want the product path in one place.
Browse [Research](Research.oot) when you want the design papers.

